		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4286&amp;p=32373#p32373"><![CDATA[
All,<br>Thanks for your replies. I solved the problem and here is what I did. <br>1. I used the TYT cable that works with all of the TYT radios that I have which includes the MD-UV380, the MD-430, and the TH-350.<br>2. I opened the Device Manager (Windows 10) and looked at the USB Com ports. I found that there were two devices named "Prolific". I looked at both of them,  one of which said that it was not working. I deleted it, made sure that the radio programming cable was not plugged in and then rebooted the PC.<br>3. After re-booting the PC, I checked Device Manager again and saw that there were no USB devices attached. I then uninstalled the OenGD77 CPS and re-installed it.<br>4. After the CPS was re-installed, I plugged the cable into the GD77, turned it on, and then plugged it into the USB port. OpenGD77 found it straightaway and I was able to convert two GD77s and on MD-UV380. The USB Com port now labels the connection as "OpenGD77".<br><br><br>Based on the above, I believe that the bad USB device driver (to which nothing was connected) was preventing the proper drivers from being installed. This problem is analogous to another post that I saw regarding USB drivers issues in the forum. Perhaps someone with more knowledge of the inner workings of Windows can elaborate.<br><br>Thanks to all who read and or commented on this thread.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=3046">n3fl</a> — Thu Aug 01, 2024 9:29 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
